Subject: [PATCH 02347/11362] [outer-loop] RE tn36: schema IS {x,y} (stale note
 CORRECTED); win + control mechanics mapped

Operator: 'reverse-engineer tn36's ACTION6 click-schema and register its adapter.'

SCHEMA (the literal ask): tn36's ACTION6 data IS {x,y} and display->grid is 1:1
(camera.display_to_grid(30,13)==(30,13)). The registry's 'schema is NOT {x,y} /
KeyError x / explorer ERRORED' note was STALE/WRONG -- the KeyError only fires for
data=None; rich_action_candidates emits {x,y} and graph_explore_solve_v2 runs CLEAN
on tn36 (no error). Corrected in the registry.

WIN MECHANIC (deep RE): tn36 = MATCH a movable object to a target on FIVE attributes
(x, y, scale, rotation, sjmtdfxdrc) -- tn36.py:2153 vklyonlcrw. L1 at reset only needs
y:13->33 (all else matches). Internal state is fully readable
(env._game.fdksqlmpki.bzirenxmrg.{htntnzkbzu,aqszntqeae}).

CONTROL: a program/transform EDITOR, not direct steering -- dispatch routes clicks to
an (empty-at-reset) tool palette / apply / object; the object carries a move-program
vkuvtkaerv=[3,0,3,0,0]; its real clickable sub-button is sxhtkytekm (9x9 @ grid 32,51).
Executing the program did NOT move the object -> the edit->execute semantics are the
remaining DELTA.

HONEST OUTCOME: did NOT register a solving adapter (would be broken without the cracked
control) -- but captured the win predicate + internal-state access + control map in the
registry so the next attempt starts from a near-complete RE, not zero. No new game
solved; the schema myth is debunked and tn36 is now well-characterized.
